1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:05,423
The brush clears a ring of metal, an armillary sphere, a model of the heavens.

2
00:00:05,423 --> 00:00:14,062
Astronomers of the Gupta age used instruments like this. One of them, Aryabhata, wrote that the Earth turns on its axis.

3
00:00:14,062 --> 00:00:17,043
Who first said that the Earth turns?

4
00:00:34,283 --> 00:00:44,939
How do we know? From the texts themselves. The Aryabhatiya was composed in 499 CE. Plays and poems of Kalidasa survive.

5
00:00:44,939 --> 00:00:48,778
Inscriptions record the patronage of Gupta kings.

6
00:01:04,517 --> 00:01:11,234
Aryabhata, born in 476 CE, wrote the Aryabhatiya in 499 CE.

7
00:01:11,234 --> 00:01:17,182
He used place value and gave a good value of pi, about three point one four one six.

8
00:01:17,182 --> 00:01:23,428
He explained eclipses as shadows, not as demons, and he wrote that the Earth rotates on its axis.

9
00:01:23,428 --> 00:01:30,362
Varahamihira wrote the Panchasiddhantika and the Brihat Samhita, on astronomy, weather and many other topics.

10
00:01:30,362 --> 00:01:34,150
The decimal system, with a symbol for zero, was in use.

11
00:01:34,150 --> 00:01:39,678
Arab scholars later carried it west, and it became known as the Hindu-Arabic numerals.

12
00:01:39,678 --> 00:01:46,950
In medicine, the Charaka Samhita and Sushruta Samhita were in use. Vagbhata compiled later work.

13
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:55,898
The age was also great in letters. Kalidasa wrote the play Abhijnanashakuntalam, and poems like the Meghaduta and Raghuvamsha.

14
00:01:55,898 --> 00:02:02,746
The Panchatantra, a book of fables, and the Amarakosha, a word list, also belong here.

15
00:02:18,483 --> 00:02:24,560
Old name, new name. The numerals we use are often called Arabic numerals.

16
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:31,937
A better name is Hindu-Arabic numerals, because the system began in India, and Arab scholars carried it west.

17
00:02:47,683 --> 00:02:58,906
Carve this tip into memory. Aryabhata: zero, pi, Earth spins;. Kalidasa: Shakuntalam. One scientist, one poet.

18
00:02:58,906 --> 00:03:01,720
Each with two works you can name.

19
00:03:17,483 --> 00:03:32,906
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Who wrote the Aryabhatiya? The answer: Aryabhata. Number two.

20
00:03:32,906 --> 00:03:45,393
Which play did Kalidasa write about a forest-born heroine? The answer: Abhijnanashakuntalam. Number three.

21
00:03:45,393 --> 00:03:57,513
Which book of fables belongs to the Gupta age? The answer: the Panchatantra. Now one line for Mains.

22
00:03:57,513 --> 00:04:02,116
Discuss the achievements of the Gupta age in science and literature.

23
00:04:21,850 --> 00:04:30,435
The armillary sphere goes into its case. It models the sky as the Gupta astronomers saw it. Three facts to take away.

24
00:04:30,435 --> 00:04:42,070
A ring model of the heavens. Aryabhata wrote in 499 CE. Zero and place value in use. Next gallery: Gupta Temples and Art.

